# The worked rules of docs/rules-design.md, in the surface.
#
# These are §8's <R1> and <R2> as printed, and a trust rule that is not in the
# document but falls out of §13: turning `the user says it is raining` into
# `it is raining` is a rule, never a hard-wired intake, so it can be argued
# with and overridden.

# §8 <R1>. `causes`, so the effect lands in a LATER moment and persists --
# water you have stopped heating stays boiled.
rule <boil> = causes(
    { +heat(?a, ?w), +water(?w) },
    { +boiling(?w) @certain, -liquid(?w) @certain } )

# §8 <R2>. `implies`, so it lands in the SAME moment and is derived. Written as
# `causes`, the backward reader would produce a plan to make it rain by making
# it cloudy -- the two-connective split is what makes that plan unwritable.
rule <weather> = implies(
    { +cloudy(?day, morning) },
    { +rain(?day, afternoon) @likely } )

# §13. The consequent is a bare variable: whatever the channel says, believe it.
# `@likely` is the rule's contribution, and the entry it deposits is graded
# min(authored, support), so a weak utterance cannot be laundered into a strong
# claim by a confident rule.
rule <trust_user> = implies(
    { +says(user, ?p) },
    { +?p @likely } )

# Standing knowledge, stamped as having come from the knowledge base.
fact +heat(anna, kettle)
fact +water(kettle)
fact +cloudy(monday, morning)

# An arrival on a channel. What is written is that the channel said so; the
# rule above is what makes it a claim about the world.
say user: +raining(here)
